# Whether an interval with one-limb bounds is the empty set (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", the row of is_empty() and "The empty set and the whole line"): x_l = +inf. The empty
# set is stored as (+inf, -inf), and every nonempty canonical interval has x_l < +inf, so the test
# of the lower bound is exact. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_empty_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m empty x x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the lower bound compared with +inf (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalPredicates.v interval_empty_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand
unused:        xu the lower bound alone tells the empty set (+inf, -inf) from a nonempty interval, whose lower bound is below +inf

m = eq(xl, const inf)
